# On the VPS this lives at /etc/fernscout/env and is read by systemd
# (EnvironmentFile= in deploy/fernscout.service). Locally, copy it to .env.
# and fill in what your deployment tier needs. Every variable here is
# optional in the sense that the app boots without it — but a capability that
# needs one and doesn't have it reports itself disabled at /api/health rather
# than pretending to work. See lib/capabilities.ts and docs/runbook.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core — every tier
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public URL this instance is served at. Used for absolute links,
# canonical tags, and push notification URLs. Falls back to content/config.json's
# site.url if unset.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Where the file store (reactions, push subscriptions) and, once the data
# layer lands, a SQLite database file live. MUST be outside the repo/image so
# a rebuild or `git pull` can never wipe it. On the VPS this is
# a named volume at /data and points DATA_DIR there.
DATA_DIR=/data

# Where the journal itself lives — every config.json, trip and photograph.
# Unset means `<repo>/content`, which is what local development wants: the
# example journal is committed, so a fresh clone renders.
#
# On a server, point this OUTSIDE the repository. Two reasons, both of which
# bite eventually. `scripts/deploy.sh` runs `git pull --ff-only`, and an agent
# writing a draft over the API writes into that same working tree — the next
# deploy then fails on local modifications. And this instance's config.json
# switches on capabilities (mail, auth) whose credentials only exist on this
# machine; committing that to the repository makes every fork fail its boot
# check. The chrome translations in `content/locales/` still fall back to the
# ones shipped in the repo, so moving this does not cost you a UI language.
# CONTENT_DIR=/var/lib/fernscout/content

# Caddy's ACME account and the domain(s) it requests certificates for.
# CADDY_DOMAIN can be a bare domain ("fernscout.ch") or include a port for
# local testing ("localhost:443" — see Caddyfile). A comma-separated list of
# domains also works.
CADDY_DOMAIN=example.com
# Let's Encrypt sends renewal/expiry notices here. Required by Caddy's ACME
# client in production.
CADDY_ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — prototype tier leaves this unset entirely (ROADMAP §2.2).
# Install Postgres on the VPS and point this at it;
# self-hosters may instead point it at any Postgres they already run.
# Local development uses `sqlite:./…` and needs no database server at all —
# see docs/plans/W06-data-layer.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://fernscout:changeme@postgres:5432/fernscout

# Only used when you create the database by hand, to initialise
# database on first boot. Keep in sync with the credentials embedded in
# DATABASE_URL above.
# POSTGRES_USER=fernscout
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme
# POSTGRES_DB=fernscout

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications (features.push in content/config.json)
# Generate once with: npm run notify -- --generate-keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth / contacts (features.auth, features.contacts) — arrive with W08/W10,
# both need DATABASE_URL set as well.
#
# CONTACTS_ENCRYPTION_KEY encrypts readers' postal addresses at rest
# (AES-256-GCM) and derives their unsubscribe links. 32 bytes, as 64 hex
# characters or base64:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Keep it out of content/ — that folder is committed. Losing it makes every
# stored address unreadable and every unsubscribe link stop working; changing
# it does the same, so treat it as permanent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET=
# CONTACTS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail (features.mail.transport) — "file" and "console" need nothing below;
# only "smtp" does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# MAIL_FROM="Fernscout <postbox@example.com>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Digest (npm run digest) — quiet rules, ROADMAP D8.
#
# Readers have a language, not a timezone, and inventing a field nobody fills
# in would be worse than guessing: "de" and "hu" readers are taken to be in
# CET/CEST, and everyone else — English is spoken in every timezone there is —
# is assumed to be near whoever runs the journal. This is that assumption.
# Defaults to Europe/Zurich.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIGEST_TIMEZONE=Europe/Zurich

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postcards / photobook print providers (features.postcards.provider,
# features.photobook.provider) — "dry-run" needs nothing below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STANNP_API_KEY=
# SWISSPOST_USERNAME=
# SWISSPOST_PASSWORD=
# PEECHO_API_KEY=
# GELATO_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ingest (npm run ingest) — see docs/ingest.md
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where untouched originals are archived. Served derivatives are stripped of
# all metadata, so this is the only copy that still carries GPS — which is
# exactly why it must live OUTSIDE content/, where no route can reach it.
# Unset means originals stay wherever you already keep them and only
# derivatives are written.
# MEDIA_ORIGINALS_DIR=/srv/fernscout-originals

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (scripts/backup.sh) — nightly dump of the database (if any),
# $DATA_DIR and content/, pushed off-VPS with restic. See docs/runbook.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A restic repository URL — a local path works for testing, but the whole
# point is that this points somewhere that is NOT this VPS: an S3-compatible
# bucket (Backblaze B2, Hetzner Object Storage, Cloudflare R2 via its S3 API),
# sftp://, or rest-server. See https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/030_preparing_a_new_repo.html
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=s3:https://s3.eu-central-003.backblazeb2.com/fernscout-backups
# RESTIC_PASSWORD=
# For S3-compatible backends:
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# How many nightly snapshots to keep before `restic forget --prune` trims them.
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=14

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ops
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set by CI/the deploy step (e.g. `export GIT_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` before
# `systemctl restart fernscout`) and read by the service at runtime;
# surfaced at /api/health so you can tell which build is actually running.
# GIT_SHA=
